                <pb facs="0627" n="499" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg499"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 355.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Langston</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-03-13"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 13,
                        1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 339.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Acknowledging the receipt of your No. 717,
                    of the 25th ultimo, respecting the case of Mr. Van Bokkelen, who is still
                    confined in the military hospital at Port-au-Prince, pending the determination
                    by the court of cassation of the question as to his right to make an assignment
                    of his property for the benefit of his creditors, I have to request you to
                    promptly inform this Department of the nature of the decision as soon as it is
                    made known by the court.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. F. BAYARD.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
